Problem: Flattening a LL
Link: https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/flattening-a-linked-list/1
T.C: 0(n^2*m) Assuming where n is total no of lists, and m is total nodes in each lists.
S.C: 0(1)

Problem Statement:

Given a linked list containing n head nodes where every node in the linked list contains two pointers:
(i) next points to the next node in the list.
(ii) bottom points to a sub-linked list where the current node is the head.
Each of the sub-linked lists nodes and the head nodes are sorted in ascending order based on their data. Flatten the linked list such that all the nodes appear in a single level while maintaining the sorted order.

Note:
1. ↓ represents the bottom pointer and → represents the next pointer.
2. The flattened list will be printed using the bottom pointer instead of the next pointer.

Examples:

Input:
5 -> 10 -> 19 -> 28
↓    ↓     ↓     ↓
7    20    22    40
↓                ↓
8                45

Output: 5 -> 7 -> 8 -> 10 -> 19 -> 20 -> 22 -> 28 -> 40 -> 45.
Explanation: 
Bottom pointer of 5 is pointing to 7.
Bottom pointer of 7 is pointing to 8.
Bottom pointer of 10 is pointing to 20 and so on.
So, after flattening the linked list the sorted list will be 
5 -> 7 -> 8 -> 10 -> 19 -> 20 -> 22 -> 28 -> 40 -> 45.
Input:
5 -> 10 -> 19 -> 28
↓          ↓     
7          22    
↓          ↓     
8          50    
↓
30

Output: 5 -> 7 -> 8 -> 10 -> 19 -> 22 -> 28 -> 30 -> 50
Explanation:
Bottom pointer of 5 is pointing to 7.
Bottom pointer of 7 is pointing to 8.
Bottom pointer of 8 is pointing to 30 and so on.
So, after flattening the linked list the sorted list will be 
5 -> 7 -> 8 -> 10 -> 19 -> 22 -> 28 -> 30 -> 50.
Constraints:
0 ≤ n ≤ 100
1 ≤ number of nodes in sub-linked list(mi) ≤ 50
1 ≤ node->data ≤ 10^4

CODE:

/*
class Node {
public:
    int data;
    Node* next;
    Node* bottom;

    Node(int x) {
        data = x;
        next = NULL;
        bottom = NULL;
    }
};
*/

class Solution {
  public:
  
    Node* Merge(Node* head1, Node* head2) {
        Node* newHead = new Node(0);
        Node* newTail = newHead;
        
        while(head1 && head2) {
            if(head1->data <= head2->data) {
                newTail->bottom = head1;
                head1 = head1->bottom;
                newTail = newTail->bottom;
                newTail->bottom = NULL;
            } else {
                newTail->bottom = head2;
                head2 = head2->bottom;
                newTail = newTail->bottom;
                newTail->bottom = NULL;
            }    
        }
        
        if(head1)
        newTail->bottom = head1;
        else
        newTail->bottom = head2;
        
        return newHead->bottom;
    }
  
    Node *flatten(Node *root) {
        // code here
        
        //1st Approach: 
        //Storing all the values into vector,
        //Sorting the vector,
        //Creating new LL from the sorted vector & return the head;
        //Extra Memory Needed.
        
        //2nd Approach:
        //In-Place Merging of nodes utilizing the sorted property of each list.
        
        Node *head1, *head2, *head3;
        while(root->next) {
            head1 = root;
            head2 = root->next;
            head3 = root->next->next;
            head1->next = NULL;
            head2->next = NULL;
            root = Merge(head1, head2);
            root->next = head3;
        }
        
        return root;
    }
};

